  "worked_solution": "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-1}+1\\,\u27f9\\,y=(x-1)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe function $y=-(x-1)^{2}+3$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2.$ So, we have $1\\le x\\le 2.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/942a39f885e6f88bef997e27914d05359be86ae1d6204f3834f0829ce4b81136.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{2}+1$ and leaves through the upper function $y=-(x-1)^{2}+3.$ So, we have $(x-1)^{2}+1\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}+1\\le y\\le -(x-1)^{2}+3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
